# Error messages of the Coordinator and of the manager

This file collects the problems of the user facing error messages of the Coordinator and of the manager (Controllers), and the rules for rewriting them.
The messages are in:
- `Coordinator/src/userMessages/Report.java` (what the user gave us and can change)
- `Coordinator/src/userMessages/Violation.java` (Fearless can no longer do its safe job)
- `Controllers/src/managerMessages/Report.java`
- `Controllers/src/managerMessages/Violation.java`

An agent finding a bad message adds it to the list below; it does not reword messages on its own.
The owner of Fearless rewrites the messages, or guides the rewriting line by line.

## Rules

- A message states what Fearless found and which rule it broke.
- A message does not tell the user what to do to fix the problem.
- A message does not speak as if Fearless knew what the user wants to do, for example as if the user used git or zip right now.
- A message may name a tool, like git or zip, that the refused file would stop from working.
- A message may say "some systems/tools" instead of a list of tools that would go out of date: "Some systems/tools trim these, which causes collisions."
- A message explains the terms it uses.
- A technical term is fine only in a message that only a technical action can reach.
- Every cause a message states is a real one, never a guess dressed as a fact.
- A message considers the most likely cause for the target user, a student who starts Fearless from its icon, not a programmer working on the command line.
- A message asking to report a problem names where: https://github.com/FearlessLang/Controllers/issues

The rules text that `Report.directFail` (and so `Report.fail`) appends (`rulesWall` in `Coordinator/src/userMessages/Report.java`) is the text of the owner, and the reference for these messages.
The "How to fix" section that `Report.fail` adds is not: it tells the user what to do, against the rules above.

## Decided

- `Report.rootNotDirectory`: the rules text does not belong to this message.
- `Report.symlinkForbidden`: "Symbolic links behave differently across systems and tools, so we forbid them" is fine.
- `Violation.freshCopyThenReport`: "If this keeps happening, report the problem" names no place: the place is https://github.com/FearlessLang/Controllers/issues
- `Violation.mustUseLauncher`: "Do not start fearless directly from the Jars." takes the cause for a programmer running the jars with Java on purpose.
  For the target user the likely cause is something changing the files of the Fearless program folder, an attack on the machine.
- `Violation.desktopUnsupported`: "headless" stays, and the message explains what headless means.
- `managerMessages.Violation.desktopHidesWindow`: "every program with decorated windows is affected, not only Fearless: on GNOME it means the process that decorates windows (mutter-x11-frames) has died" is a guess, not a known cause.
- `managerMessages.Violation.couldNotUseInstanceLock`: "This feature is called file locking." is good.

## Listed, not yet discussed

- `Report.pathTooLong`: the length is not shown.
- `Report.multiDotExtNotAllowed`: "more than one dot in the extension part"; "Most files".
- `Report.generatedTestNameReserved`: "is almost certainly an accident".
- `Report.zipNoEntries`: "This is most likely a mistake."
- `Report.emptyDirectory`, `Report.zipEmptyDirectoryEntry`: "they may not be supported by compression tools (zip)"; zip stores empty folders, and the second message is about an entry already in a zip.
- `Report.zipNestingTooDeep`: "if recursively unzipped, it would clearly go over the OS path length limit".
- `Report.zipDuplicateEntryName`: "(Schizophrenic ZIP file)"; "different content is seen in different moments".
- `Report.dateInTheFuture`: "This usually comes from ..." lists likely causes, then "Save the file again".
- `Report.zipBadEntryName`: lists every invalid pattern instead of the one the entry breaks.
- `Report.invisibleInvalidSurrogate`: "contains invalid Unicode" does not show the character.
- `Violation.badLaunchArg`: "a broken path" does not say what is broken.
- `managerMessages.Violation.managerFolderIntro`: the manager process and the processes of the mains, explained again in each message that calls `managerFolderIntro`.
- `managerMessages.Violation.blockingPrograms` and "Common fixes are ..." in `couldNotUseInstanceLock`: guessed programs and fixes.
- `managerMessages.Violation.newFilesNotReported`: "Put this Fearless folder on a disk of this computer."
- `managerMessages.Report.symbolicLink`: "This path is a symbolic link/junction/mount point, and Fearless does not follow symbolic links/junctions/mount points:" then the path.
- `managerMessages.Report.folderUnreadable`: "Give Fearless access to it, or forget this project."
